  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Name the energy stores.', 'Kinetic, gravitational potential, elastic potential, thermal, chemical, magnetic, electrostatic and nuclear.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Name four ways energy can be transferred.', 'Mechanically (by forces), electrically, by heating, and by radiation (light and sound).', 2),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is the equation for kinetic energy?', 'KE = ½mv².', 3),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is the equation for gravitational potential energy?', 'GPE = mgh.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Calculate the KE of a 2 kg ball moving at 3 m/s.', '½ × 2 × 3² = 9 J.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Calculate the GPE gained by a 5 kg mass lifted 2 m (g = 10 N/kg).', '5 × 10 × 2 = 100 J.', 6),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is the law of conservation of energy?', 'Energy can''t be created or destroyed, only transferred between stores.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is wasted energy?', 'Energy transferred to stores that aren''t useful, usually thermal energy in the surroundings.', 8),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is efficiency?', 'Useful energy output ÷ total energy input (× 100 for a percentage).', 9),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'A motor uses 200 J and transfers 150 J usefully. What is its efficiency?', '150 ÷ 200 = 0.75, or 75%.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'How can energy waste be reduced?', 'Lubrication to reduce friction, and insulation to reduce heat loss.', 11),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is thermal conductivity?', 'How quickly a material transfers energy by heating through conduction.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'How does the thickness of a wall affect heat loss?', 'Thicker walls reduce the rate of heat loss.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is a Sankey diagram?', 'A diagram showing energy transfers, with arrow widths proportional to the amount of energy.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What are non-renewable energy resources?', 'Resources that will run out, e.g. coal, oil, gas and nuclear fuel.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What are renewable energy resources?', 'Resources that will be replenished, e.g. solar, wind, hydroelectric, tidal, geothermal, biofuel and wave.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of fossil fuels.', 'Advantage: reliable and can meet demand. Disadvantage: produce CO₂ and will run out.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of wind power.', 'Advantage: renewable, no CO₂ during operation. Disadvantage: unreliable and can be noisy or unsightly.', 18),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of solar power.', 'Advantage: renewable, no emissions. Disadvantage: no power at night and less in cloudy weather.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of nuclear power.', 'Advantage: no CO₂ and reliable. Disadvantage: radioactive waste and risk of accidents.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of hydroelectric power.', 'Advantage: reliable, renewable and responds quickly. Disadvantage: floods valleys and destroys habitats.', 21),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Give an advantage and disadvantage of tidal power.', 'Advantage: reliable and predictable. Disadvantage: few suitable sites and affects habitats.', 22),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Why are fossil fuels still used?', 'They are reliable, the infrastructure already exists, and they can respond quickly to demand.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is a biofuel?', 'A fuel made from plant or animal matter; it is renewable and can be carbon-neutral.', 24),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is geothermal energy?', 'Energy from hot rocks underground, used to heat water and generate electricity.', 25),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What happens to energy when a ball is dropped?', 'GPE is transferred to KE; some is transferred to thermal energy by air resistance.', 26),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What happens to energy in a pendulum?', 'Energy is continuously transferred between GPE and KE, with some lost to the surroundings.', 27),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'How does a lubricant improve efficiency?', 'It reduces friction between moving parts, so less energy is wasted as heat.', 28),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'What is the trend in UK energy use?', 'Increasing use of renewables and decreasing use of coal.', 29),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '3', 'Conservation of Energy', 'Why is it hard to replace fossil fuels quickly?', 'Cost of new infrastructure, the reliability of supply, and public objections to some renewables.', 30),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is a wave?', 'A disturbance that transfers energy from one place to another without transferring matter.', 1),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is a transverse wave?', 'A wave where the vibrations are at right angles to the direction of energy transfer, e.g. light.', 2),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is a longitudinal wave?', 'A wave where the vibrations are parallel to the direction of energy transfer, e.g. sound.', 3),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is amplitude?', 'The maximum displacement of a point on the wave from its rest position.', 4),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is wavelength?', 'The distance from one point on a wave to the same point on the next wave.', 5),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is frequency?', 'The number of waves passing a point each second, measured in hertz (Hz).', 6),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is the period of a wave?', 'The time for one complete wave to pass a point: T = 1/f.', 7),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is the wave equation?', 'Wave speed = frequency × wavelength (v = fλ).', 8),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'A wave has frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 2 m. What is its speed?', '50 × 2 = 100 m/s.', 9),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is the equation linking speed, distance and time for waves?', 'v = x/t.', 10),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What are compressions and rarefactions?', 'Regions in a longitudinal wave where particles are close together (compressions) and spread apart (rarefactions).', 11),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is reflection?', 'When a wave bounces off a surface.', 12),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is refraction?', 'The change in direction of a wave as it passes from one medium to another, due to a change in speed.', 13),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'Why do waves refract?', 'They change speed when entering a different medium.', 14),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What happens to light entering glass from air?', 'It slows down and bends towards the normal.', 15),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is the normal?', 'An imaginary line at 90° to the surface where the wave hits.', 16),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'How can the speed of sound in air be measured?', 'Measure the time for sound to travel a known distance, e.g. using an echo or two microphones, then use v = d/t.', 17),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'How can the speed of water waves be measured in a ripple tank?', 'Measure the wavelength using a strobe or ruler and count the frequency, then use v = fλ.', 18),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is the range of human hearing?', 'About 20 Hz to 20,000 Hz.', 19),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is ultrasound?', 'Sound with a frequency above 20,000 Hz.', 20),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'What is infrasound?', 'Sound with a frequency below 20 Hz.', 21),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'How is ultrasound used?', 'In medical imaging (e.g. prenatal scans), sonar, and cleaning.', 22),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'How is sonar used?', 'Ultrasound pulses are sent out and the time taken for echoes to return is used to calculate distance.', 23),
  ('Physics', 'Edexcel', 'gcse', '4', 'Waves', 'How are seismic waves used to study the Earth?', 'P-waves and S-waves travel through the Earth at different speeds; S-waves can''t travel through liquid, showing the outer core is liquid.', 24),
